## Phase 5: The "After-Action"

> Handles what happens once a winner is declared. Two responsibilities: persist to Postgres, and display the result.

### Step 5.1 — Persistence Trigger (in [RacePage](file:///c:/Users/rnasy/Desktop/Typeracing/src/app/race/%5BroomId%5D/page.tsx#23-224))

**Rationale:** The save call lives in [RacePage](file:///c:/Users/rnasy/Desktop/Typeracing/src/app/race/%5BroomId%5D/page.tsx#23-224), not inside [useRaceSync](file:///c:/Users/rnasy/Desktop/Typeracing/src/hooks/useRaceSync.ts#23-103), because:
1. It's a one-shot action (not a polling concern)
2. It needs access to `persistenceStatus` state for the modal
3. It must be idempotent — `useRef` prevents double-fires from React strict mode

#### Implementation Logic

```typescript
// RacePage.tsx
const hasSaved = useRef(false);

useEffect(() => {
  if (gameState !== 'FINISHED' || hasSaved.current) return;
  hasSaved.current = true;
  
  const persist = async () => {
    setGameState({ persistenceStatus: 'SAVING' });
    try {
      const result = await multiplayerApi.saveResults(roomId, userId);
      // BOTH are success states (Persistence Idempotency rule)
      setGameState({ 
        persistenceStatus: result.status === 'SAVED' || result.status === 'ALREADY_SAVED' 
          ? 'SAVED' 
          : 'ERROR' 
      });
    } catch {
      setGameState({ persistenceStatus: 'ERROR' });
    }
  };
  
  persist();
}, [gameState]);
```

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Why `useRef` and not `useState`:** React 18 strict mode can double-fire effects. A ref survives re-renders without causing re-execution. This is the only correct pattern for fire-once side effects.

